## 10. Retrieval Strategy — Detailed

### PDF: Vectorless Page-Tree Index

```
PDF Upload
   |
   v
PyMuPDF: extract page-by-page text
   |
   v
page_index = {1: "...", 2: "...", ...}  (stored in graph state / session_state)
   |
   v
ClauseExtractAgent iterates pages in order
   |  - groups consecutive pages into "sections" by heading heuristics
   |  - passes each section to LLM with extract_clauses prompt
   v
ClauseCard list  (section_reference = "Page 3-4")
```

**Why vectorless for PDF?**
- PDFs have natural page structure → reliable, human-readable citations ("Page 3")
- Clause extraction from structured pages is more accurate than from arbitrary semantic chunks
- Avoids the 90 MB model download for the most common input type

### URL/Text: FAISS + HuggingFace Embeddings

```
URL: trafilatura scrape, or Text paste
   |
   v
Chunker (overlap-aware, ~512 token chunks)
   |
   v
HuggingFace all-MiniLM-L6-v2: embed each chunk into float32 vectors
   |
   v
FAISS IndexFlatL2 built in memory
   |
   v  (stored as bytes in session_state — no disk write needed)
   |
For clause extraction:
   - Query FAISS with each "find clauses about X" query
   - Top-K chunks passed to ClauseExtractAgent
For Q&A:
   - Query FAISS with user question
   - Top-K chunks passed to QAAgent as context
```

**Session-scoped only:** The FAISS index is never written to disk. It is serialized to bytes with `faiss.serialize_index()`, stored in `session_state`, and discarded when the session ends — satisfying the no-persistence requirement.

